# secrets.env.example — every environment variable this system reads, by name.
#
# REPO-BASELINE.md §2: "Commit a secrets.env.example documenting EVERY variable with
# three things: its name, its tier, and where its value comes from." A variable whose
# provenance is undocumented has no one answer to "where does this value come from".
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS A VALUE.
#
# Every variable below is NAMED. Where a value appears it is a placeholder form — $VAR,
# <description>, or a public address that is already in a committed fly.toml. If you find
# yourself about to paste a real value here, that is the failure this file exists to
# prevent, and .gitleaks.toml is deliberately NOT allowlisted on this path: a value in
# this file would be a leak with a README wrapped round it, and the scanner will say so.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE SECRET'S JOURNEY — one chain, documented once (REPO-BASELINE.md §3)
#
#   local store  ->  AppHost parameter  ->  environment variable  ->  config key
#
#   dotnet user-secrets        builder.AddParameter(       .WithEnvironment(        configuration[
#     --project src/             "auth-signing-key",         "Jwt__PrivateKeyPem",    "Jwt:PrivateKeyPem"]
#     AbOvo.AppHost              secret: true)               authSigningKey)
#     set "Parameters:
#     auth-signing-key" ...
#
# Two things about that chain are worth reading twice.
#
#   The DOUBLE UNDERSCORE is not decoration. .NET maps `Jwt__Issuer` in the environment to
#   the configuration key `Jwt:Issuer`, because a colon is not legal in an environment
#   variable name on every platform. A single underscore does NOT map, and the symptom is
#   a service that starts perfectly and behaves as though the setting were absent.
#
#   `__0` is an ARRAY INDEX. `Cors__AllowedOrigins__0` is the first element of
#   `Cors:AllowedOrigins`. A second origin is `__1`. There is no comma-separated form, and
#   a comma-separated value binds as ONE origin that matches nothing.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE FIVE TIERS
#
#   always    Required for the service to be CORRECT. A deployment missing one of these
#             is misconfigured, whether or not it starts.
#   mode      Selects behaviour. Absent, the service takes a documented default — usually
#             the degraded or the development one, which is right locally and wrong in
#             production.
#   secret    A credential. Never in a file in the working tree, never in a [env] block,
#             never in a commit. Local: `dotnet user-secrets`. Deployed: `fly secrets set`.
#             CI: a GitHub Environment secret.
#   ci        Read by a workflow, not by a running service. Lives in the GitHub
#             Environment named `dev`.
#   tuning    Changes a number, not a behaviour. Every one has a working default.
#
# These map onto REPO-BASELINE §2's three (always required / required in CI / optional) as
# always+secret -> always required, ci -> required in CI, mode+tuning -> optional. The five
# are used here because "optional" hides the distinction that matters most in practice:
# a `mode` variable left unset does not fail, it silently picks the other behaviour.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT DEGRADES WITHOUT IT (P8)
#
# Every entry below carries a `without it:` line. That is the point of the file: skipping
# an integration has to be an INFORMED decision, which means knowing exactly which feature
# stops working. A fresh clone with every optional integration skipped still runs — that is
# a property of this scaffold, not an aspiration, and `bash scripts/setup.sh` is where it
# is arranged.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#
#   You do not copy it to `.env` and fill it in for local development. Local development
#   reads `dotnet user-secrets`, which `bash scripts/setup.sh` initialises — the local
#   store, never files in the working tree, because a secret in the tree is one
#   `git add -A` from being history.
#
#   `.env` exists for one purpose: the local CI-mirror scripts, which read from it and
#   never from inline literals. `.env` and `.env.*` are gitignored from the first commit.
#
#   For a deployed environment, flyio/SECRETS.md is authoritative: it names the five root
#   secrets and says which are ASSEMBLED rather than stored.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE AUTHORITATIVE SOURCE PER VARIABLE (REPO-BASELINE.md §8)
#
# The same topology is described in three places, and they are not equals. For each
# variable below, `set in:` names the ONE place that decides it. The estate's recorded
# drift was found exactly here — a variable present in the dev branch of a composition
# root and missing from the publish branch.
#
#   src/AbOvo.AppHost/AppHost.cs   authoritative for LOCAL development
#   flyio/*.fly.toml  [env]        authoritative for DEPLOYED non-secret configuration
#   .github/workflows/flyio.yml    authoritative for DEPLOYED secrets (fly secrets set)
#
# =====================================================================================


# =====================================================================================
#  1.  AbOvo.Api  —  the backend service
# =====================================================================================

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ConnectionStrings__apidb
#   tier:       secret
#   set in:     .github/workflows/flyio.yml (deployed) / AppHost WithReference (local)
#   value:      Host=ab-ovo-postgres.internal;Port=5432;Database=apidb;Username=abovo_api;Password=$APIDB_PASSWORD
#   without it: the API starts anyway and falls back to an in-memory database. /health
#               reports the degradation rather than refusing to boot (P8). Everything a
#               reader writes is lost on the next restart, and nothing in the logs says
#               so — the service looks healthy because it is healthy, at being useless.
#               `.internal`, never `.flycast` and never a public address: a database is
#               reached over 6PN and has no public listener.
ConnectionStrings__apidb=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_PROVIDER
#   tier:       mode
#   set in:     src/AbOvo.AppHost/AppHost.cs (local) / flyio/api.fly.toml [env] (deployed)
#   values:     PostgreSQL | SqlServer
#   without it: InMemory. The provider is a configuration switch and not a compile-time
#               decision (P4) — which is what lets the test suite and a fresh clone run
#               with no container at all. Set to PostgreSQL wherever a connection string
#               exists; a connection string with no provider is the combination that
#               silently ignores the database you just configured.
DATABASE_PROVIDER=PostgreSQL

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jwt__Authority
#   tier:       always  (in any deployment that has users)
#   set in:     AppHost (local, resolves to the authservice container) / api.fly.toml
#   value:      https://ab-ovo-authservice-dev.fly.dev
#   without it: authentication is not configured. Anonymous endpoints keep serving and
#               authenticated ones answer 401 rather than 500 — P8's degraded path, and it
#               is tested. /health names it. The API holds no key material either way (P5):
#               it validates RS256 against the published JWKS and never mints a token.
Jwt__Authority=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jwt__Issuer
#   tier:       always
#   set in:     AppHost (AbOvoIdentity.Issuer) / api.fly.toml / authservice's own env
#   value:      AbOvo
#   without it: no scheme is configured, so every authenticated request is 401.
#               PRODUCT-SPECIFIC ON PURPOSE. authservice defaults both issuer and audience
#               to the bare string "AuthService"; two products both on the defaults would
#               accept each other's tokens. Every deployment is meant to be an independent
#               trust root.
#               Note this is a BARE STRING and not a URL. A full OIDC discovery client
#               will reject it for that reason — see AB_OVO_JWT_ISSUER below.
Jwt__Issuer=AbOvo

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jwt__Audience
#   tier:       always
#   set in:     as Jwt__Issuer
#   value:      AbOvo
#   without it: 401 on every authenticated request.
#               Validate it STRICTLY AND EXACTLY. authservice issues 2FA challenge tokens
#               with audience "AbOvo:2fa", signed with the SAME key — so a validator that
#               checks the signature and skips the audience accepts a half-authenticated
#               token as a session.
Jwt__Audience=AbOvo

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jwt__RequireHttpsMetadata
#   tier:       mode
#   set in:     src/AbOvo.AppHost/AppHost.cs — LOCAL ONLY, and never in a fly.toml
#   values:     true (default) | false
#   without it: true, which is correct everywhere except the AppHost, whose own endpoint
#               is plain http and whose metadata fetch would therefore fail. Setting this
#               to false in a deployed environment means key discovery over a channel
#               anyone on the path can rewrite, which is the whole trust root.
Jwt__RequireHttpsMetadata=false

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cors__AllowedOrigins__0
#   tier:       always
#   set in:     AppHost (http://localhost:3000) / api.fly.toml (the deployed web origin)
#   value:      https://ab-ovo-web-dev.fly.dev
#   without it: no cross-origin request from the browser reaches the API, so the reader
#               loads and every call fails — visible only in the browser console.
#               LAUNCH BLOCKER 1: "production CORS origins never configured; only
#               localhost:3000 was ever set." It ships unchanged because nothing fails
#               locally. Named explicit origins only: never wildcard a PaaS apex such as
#               *.fly.dev, and never combine such a wildcard with credentials — that
#               trusts every tenant of the platform.
Cors__AllowedOrigins__0=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network__ClientIpHeader
#   tier:       mode
#   set in:     flyio/api.fly.toml [env] and flyio/authservice.fly.toml [env]
#   value:      Fly-Client-IP
#   without it: defaults to Fly-Client-IP, which is right on Fly and wrong behind any
#               other proxy. Paired with the next variable, which is what decides whether
#               it is read at all.
Network__ClientIpHeader=Fly-Client-IP

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network__TrustProxyClientIpHeader
#   tier:       mode
#   set in:     flyio/api.fly.toml [env] — true when deployed, unset locally
#   values:     true | false (default)
#   without it: false, so the rate limiter keys on the socket peer. Behind Fly every TCP
#               peer IS the edge proxy, so EVERY USER SHARES ONE RATE-LIMIT BUCKET — and
#               it fails silently, because the limiter works perfectly on the key it was
#               given. The mirror-image error is as bad: set it true with nothing in
#               front and the header is client-supplied, so every visitor picks their own
#               bucket. It is a deployment fact, never an inference.
Network__TrustProxyClientIpHeader=false

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Swagger__Enabled
#   tier:       mode
#   set in:     src/AbOvo.Api/appsettings.Development.json (true, development only)
#   values:     true | false
#   without it: off outside Development. KEEP IT OFF IN PRODUCTION. It is information
#               disclosure — the route map, the DTO shapes and the validation rules,
#               gift-wrapped. The recorded pattern is that it gets enabled "for deployment
#               debugging" in one document and filed as a finding by the next review.
Swagger__Enabled=false

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT
#   tier:       tuning        (optional — needed for traces and metrics leaving the process)
#   set in:     the Aspire dashboard injects it locally; unset in fly.toml today
#   value:      http://localhost:4317
#   without it: OpenTelemetry instrumentation still runs and still records; nothing is
#               EXPORTED. Logs remain on stdout and are still readable with `fly logs`.
#               Nothing fails, which is exactly why it goes unnoticed: the only symptom is
#               a dashboard that stays empty.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT
#   tier:       mode
#   set in:     flyio/api.fly.toml [env]. Deliberately NOT in the Dockerfile — nothing
#               environment-specific belongs in the image; that is what makes one image
#               promotable across environments.
#   values:     Development | Production
#   without it: Production. Development additionally loads appsettings.Development.json,
#               which is where Swagger is switched on.
ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ASPNETCORE_URLS
#   tier:       always (already set in src/AbOvo.Api/Dockerfile)
#   value:      http://+:8080
#   without it: Kestrel binds its own default, which is not 8080, and the Fly proxy
#               reaches nothing. `+` binds every interface: a container bound to localhost
#               is unreachable from the proxy, and the symptom is a deploy that hangs and
#               then fails its health checks. Must equal `internal_port` in api.fly.toml.
ASPNETCORE_URLS=http://+:8080

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logging__LogLevel__Default
#   tier:       tuning
#   values:     Trace | Debug | Information (default) | Warning | Error
#   without it: Information, from appsettings.json.
Logging__LogLevel__Default=Information


# =====================================================================================
#  2.  authservice  —  the identity service, adopted from its PUBLISHED image
# =====================================================================================
#
# ghcr.io/konradcinkusz/authservice:v0.3.1 — pinned, never :latest, source never vendored.
#
# NOT v0.1.0. Every example in the upstream documentation shows v0.1.0, and RS256, the
# JWKS endpoint and the discovery document DO NOT EXIST before v0.3.0. Following those
# examples leaves the shared-secret model P5 forbids, in which verify = mint.
#
# Health, and this is the one to get right: /health and /alive are LIVENESS — they answer
# 200 as soon as Kestrel binds. /health/ready is READINESS and answers 503 until schema
# initialisation has finished. POINT THE PLATFORM CHECK AT /health/ready. A liveness check
# used as readiness declares the service up while every request to it fails.
#
# min_machines_running MUST be 1. Every validator fetches the JWKS in-request, so a scaled
# -to-zero identity service is a cold start inside somebody's login.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ConnectionStrings__DefaultConnection
#   tier:       secret
#   set in:     .github/workflows/flyio.yml, ASSEMBLED from a password plus a known host
#   value:      Host=ab-ovo-postgres.internal;Port=5432;Database=authdb;Username=abovo_auth;Password=$AUTHDB_PASSWORD
#   without it: the service cannot reach its user store. authdb only — it gets no
#               credential for apidb, and the API gets none for authdb (P3). The logical
#               boundary is enforced in SQL by postgres.fly.toml, not by these names.
ConnectionStrings__DefaultConnection=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jwt__PrivateKeyPem
#   tier:       secret          <-- THE single mandatory secret in this system
#   set in:     .github/workflows/flyio.yml from the `dev` environment secret
#               JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM; locally by `bash scripts/setup.sh`, which GENERATES
#               it rather than asking anyone to invent one.
#   value:      a PKCS#8 RSA key, >= 2048 bits, both header lines included:
#                 openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048
#               `genpkey`, not `genrsa`: genrsa emits PKCS#1 ("BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY"),
#               which is the wrong container, and the header alone tells you which you have.
#   without it: the service boots on an EPHEMERAL key and looks perfectly healthy while
#               invalidating every token it ever issued, on every restart. Worse with
#               Jwt__Algorithm unset: a missing key silently infers HS256 and publishes an
#               EMPTY JWKS — green health checks, and every downstream validator rejecting
#               every token. A missing signing key must STOP a deploy, not degrade it.
#   verify:     curl -fsS https://ab-ovo-authservice-dev.fly.dev/.well-known/jwks.json \
#                 | jq -e '.keys | length > 0'
#               An empty `keys` array is a green deployment nobody can log in to.
Jwt__PrivateKeyPem=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jwt__Algorithm
#   tier:       always        <-- set it EXPLICITLY; this is not a default worth inheriting
#   set in:     src/AbOvo.AppHost/AppHost.cs and flyio/authservice.fly.toml [env]
#   value:      RS256
#   without it: INFERRED, and the inference is HS256 when the key is missing — which
#               publishes an empty JWKS and mints tokens with a shared secret. That is the
#               exact model P5 exists to forbid: a symmetric secret shared between two
#               services means verify = mint.
Jwt__Algorithm=RS256

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jwt__Issuer / Jwt__Audience   (authservice's own copies)
#   tier:       always
#   value:      AbOvo / AbOvo
#   without it: the default "AuthService", which collides with every other product on the
#               default. Both sides must agree: authservice mints with these, the API
#               validates against the Jwt__Issuer and Jwt__Audience in section 1.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jwt__PublicBaseUrl
#   tier:       always
#   set in:     flyio/authservice.fly.toml [env]
#   value:      https://ab-ovo-authservice-dev.fly.dev
#   without it: the discovery document and the JWKS advertise an address that is not the
#               one clients reach, so key discovery fails from outside the private network
#               while working perfectly from inside it.
Jwt__PublicBaseUrl=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database__SchemaMode
#   tier:       mode
#   set in:     src/AbOvo.AppHost/AppHost.cs and flyio/authservice.fly.toml [env]
#   value:      EnsureCreated
#   without it: schema initialisation does not run and /health/ready never clears.
#               `DATABASE_SCHEMA_MODE` IS DEAD — it is read by nothing and never works.
#               The variable is `Database__SchemaMode`, with the double underscore.
Database__SchemaMode=EnsureCreated

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cors__AllowedOrigins__0   (authservice's own copy)
#   tier:       always
#   value:      https://ab-ovo-web-dev.fly.dev      (locally: http://localhost:3000)
#   without it: the browser cannot call the login endpoints, so sign-in fails with a CORS
#               error and nothing server-side reports a problem.
#               `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGIN` DOES NOT EXIST. The array-index form is the only one
#               that binds; a second origin is `__1`.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network__ClientIpHeader   (authservice's own copy)
#   tier:       mode
#   value:      Fly-Client-IP
#   without it: every user shares one rate-limit bucket, on the login endpoint — which is
#               the one endpoint where that matters most. LAUNCH BLOCKER 3: rate limiting
#               present in MOST services is the normal failure mode, and the unprotected
#               one is precisely the target.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT   (authservice's own copy)
#   tier:       mode
#   value:      Production                (locally: Development)


# =====================================================================================
#  3.  web  —  the Next.js reader and BFF
# =====================================================================================
#
# Read SERVER-SIDE at RUN time, per request, in /api/config. NEVER NEXT_PUBLIC_*: those
# are inlined into the client bundle at build time, which costs one image per environment
# and breaks build-once-deploy-many. There is no build argument for an address anywhere in
# web/app/Dockerfile, and that is what makes the image promotable unchanged.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AB_OVO_API_URL
#   tier:       always (deployed) / injected by the AppHost (local)
#   set in:     flyio/web.fly.toml [env] / AppHost .WithEnvironment
#   value:      http://ab-ovo-api-dev.internal:8080
#   without it: the BFF falls back to Aspire's own service-discovery variables
#               (services__api__http__0). With neither, every proxied call fails and the
#               reader loop still works — Phase 1 is designed to run with no account and
#               no backend, which is what makes that a degradation rather than an outage.
AB_OVO_API_URL=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AB_OVO_AUTH_URL
#   tier:       always (deployed)
#   set in:     flyio/web.fly.toml [env] / AppHost .WithEnvironment
#   value:      http://ab-ovo-authservice-dev.internal:8080
#   without it: sign-in is unavailable; anonymous reading is not affected.
AB_OVO_AUTH_URL=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AB_OVO_AUTH_PUBLIC_URL
#   tier:       mode      (optional — needed when the browser must reach the issuer directly)
#   value:      https://ab-ovo-authservice-dev.fly.dev
#   without it: the server-side address is reused. That is correct when every auth call is
#               proxied and wrong the moment the browser is expected to reach the issuer
#               itself, because a `.internal` address does not resolve from a laptop.
AB_OVO_AUTH_PUBLIC_URL=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AB_OVO_JWT_ISSUER / AB_OVO_JWT_AUDIENCE
#   tier:       mode
#   values:     AbOvo / AbOvo   (the defaults, which is why they are usually unset)
#   without it: "AbOvo" both. They exist so the strings are configurable rather than
#               compiled in.
#               THE ISSUER IS A BARE STRING, NOT A URL. A full OIDC discovery client
#               REJECTS a non-URL issuer. In Node the working shape is `jose` with
#               createRemoteJWKSet pointed straight at the jwks.json URL, treating `iss`
#               as an opaque string — not a discovery client.
AB_OVO_JWT_ISSUER=AbOvo
AB_OVO_JWT_AUDIENCE=AbOvo

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AB_OVO_ENVIRONMENT
#   tier:       tuning
#   without it: NODE_ENV, then "unknown". Cosmetic: it is what /api/config reports.
AB_OVO_ENVIRONMENT=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AB_OVO_PROXY_TIMEOUT_MS
#   tier:       tuning
#   without it: the built-in default. Raise it only with a reason; a long timeout turns a
#               dead backend into a slow frontend, which is harder to diagnose than an error.
AB_OVO_PROXY_TIMEOUT_MS=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PORT / HOSTNAME
#   tier:       always (already set in web/app/Dockerfile)
#   values:     3000 / 0.0.0.0
#   without it: the standalone server binds localhost, which is unreachable from the Fly
#               proxy — a health check that never passes with an application log that
#               looks fine.
PORT=3000
HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NODE_ENV / NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED
#   tier:       tuning (both set in the Dockerfile)
NODE_ENV=production
NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1


# =====================================================================================
#  4.  CI and deployment  —  the GitHub Environment named `dev`
# =====================================================================================
#
# FIVE ROOT SECRETS, AND NOTHING ELSE. Everything the estate needs beyond these is
# DERIVED: connection strings are assembled from a password plus a known host rather than
# stored per service. A secret set by hand on one app and forgotten is how environments
# drift.
#
# They live in a GitHub ENVIRONMENT (Settings -> Environments -> dev), not in repository
# secrets. An environment can carry required reviewers, a branch restriction and a
# deployment history; a repository secret can carry none of those and is readable by every
# workflow in the repo.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FLY_API_TOKEN
#   tier:       ci + secret
#   value:      fly tokens create org --name "ab-ovo github actions" --expiry 8760h
#   without it: every flyctl call in every workflow fails. Nothing deploys, scales or is
#               destroyed. This one token can do all three to every app in the estate,
#               which is why it is scoped and dated rather than permanent.
FLY_API_TOKEN=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM
#   tier:       ci + secret
#   value:      the PKCS#8 RSA key from section 2, WHOLE, both header lines included
#   without it: the deploy must fail rather than continue — see Jwt__PrivateKeyPem.
#               A GitHub secret preserves newlines; a PEM with its newlines stripped is
#               not parseable and the service starts anyway on an inferred HS256.
#               When setting it: QUOTE the argument. Unquoted, the shell splits the key
#               into twenty-odd arguments and flyctl takes the first line as the value.
JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD / APIDB_PASSWORD / AUTHDB_PASSWORD
#   tier:       ci + secret
#   value:      openssl rand -hex 32        (run three times)
#               -hex rather than -base64 on purpose: the output is [0-9a-f] only, so it
#               needs no escaping inside a connection string, inside the SQL literal the
#               init script builds, or inside a shell argument. A base64 password
#               containing + or / is legal everywhere and looks fine right up until
#               something quotes it differently.
#   without it: no database roles and no application databases.
#               ORDERING MATTERS, ONCE: the two role passwords are read by the first-boot
#               init script, and the postgres entrypoint runs it ONLY when the data
#               directory is empty. Stage them BEFORE the first deploy. Afterwards you get
#               a healthy Postgres with no application databases in it, and the recovery is
#               to destroy the volume and redeploy.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
APIDB_PASSWORD=
AUTHDB_PASSWORD=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GITLEAKS_LICENSE
#   tier:       ci        (optional — needed only if this repository moves to an ORGANISATION)
#   without it: nothing. gitleaks-action is free for personal accounts and public
#               repositories, which is what github.com/konradcinkusz/ab-ovo is. The name is
#               referenced and unset so that the day the repo moves under an organisation,
#               the failure reads as "buy a license" rather than as a scanner that
#               mysteriously stopped working.
GITLEAKS_LICENSE=

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GITHUB_TOKEN
#   tier:       ci        (provided automatically by GitHub Actions; never set by hand)
#   without it: not applicable. Listed because it appears in workflow files and would
#               otherwise look like something somebody has to configure.


# =====================================================================================
#  5.  Local development  —  the user-secrets store
# =====================================================================================
#
# These are NOT environment variables and NOT lines in a .env. They are keys in the local
# secret store, which lives outside the working tree — on Linux and macOS under
# ~/.microsoft/usersecrets/<UserSecretsId>/secrets.json, on Windows under %APPDATA%.
# That is the whole point: a secret in the tree is one `git add -A` from being history.
#
#   dotnet user-secrets --project src/AbOvo.AppHost set "Parameters:auth-signing-key" "<pem>"
#   dotnet user-secrets --project src/AbOvo.AppHost set "Parameters:auth-db-password"  "<password>"
#
# `Parameters:<name>` is the key shape Aspire's AddParameter(name, secret: true) reads.
# The UserSecretsId is `ab-ovo-apphost` (src/AbOvo.AppHost/AbOvo.AppHost.csproj); the API
# has its own store under `ab-ovo-api`.
#
#   Parameters:auth-signing-key   tier: secret
#                                 GENERATED by `bash scripts/setup.sh` step 3. Reaches the
#                                 authservice container as Jwt__PrivateKeyPem.
#                                 without it: `dotnet run --project src/AbOvo.AppHost`
#                                 stops and asks for a value it cannot invent.
#
#   Parameters:auth-db-password   tier: secret
#                                 GENERATED by `bash scripts/setup.sh` step 3. Becomes the
#                                 password of the local Postgres container, and therefore
#                                 half of both assembled connection strings.
#                                 without it: the same prompt, and no database.
